-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 58: re-assert the `relation` table as TYPE RELATION (Track O.1,
-- made NON-DESTRUCTIVE in O.2a).
--
-- Schema-drift remediation: on a drifted DB the `relation` table is a
-- pre-migration-39 NORMAL table (legacy subject/object/predicate shape, a few
-- malformed null-endpoint rows). Because the table already existed as NORMAL,
-- migration 39's `DEFINE TABLE relation ... TYPE RELATION` never took effect, so
-- every `RELATE $a->relation->$b` fails with
--   "Found record ... which is not a relation, but expected a NORMAL"
-- and the KG can never store relations.
--
-- HISTORY / why this file was edited. The original migration 58 used
-- `REMOVE TABLE IF EXISTS relation` before redefining. That converts the kind
-- but is DESTRUCTIVE: on an already-healthy environment that holds real graph
-- edges (e.g. a dev who built KG edges between migration 39 and 58) it silently
-- wipes them all. The runner applies migrations in ascending version order, so
-- this destructive 58 runs BEFORE the non-destructive 62 on any environment
-- currently at version <= 57 — 62 cannot protect edges 58 already deleted.
--
-- This file is now rewritten to the SAME non-destructive approach migration 62
-- uses (DELETE only null-endpoint junk, then DEFINE TABLE OVERWRITE). Rationale
-- for editing an already-applied migration:
--   * Environments already at version >= 58 have 58 recorded in
--     `_sbl_migrations` and will NOT re-run it (the runner's version gate), so
--     this edit cannot change their state — for them, 62 re-asserts the correct
--     definition anyway.
--   * Only environments still at version <= 57 will execute 58, and those are
--     exactly the ones we must protect from edge loss.
-- So the edit is both safe (no replay on advanced envs) and correct (protects
-- the at-risk population). 62 remains the canonical idempotent remediation and
-- supersedes this file going forward.
-- ============================================================================

-- 1. Drop ONLY null-endpoint junk rows (the drifted legacy records). Real graph
--    edges always have non-null in/out, so this never touches them.
DELETE relation WHERE in = NONE OR out = NONE;

-- 2. Re-assert the canonical edge definition with OVERWRITE. On SurrealDB
--    >= 2.1.4 OVERWRITE converts a drifted NORMAL table to RELATION and
--    PRESERVES existing edge records on an already-healthy RELATION table
--    (it rewrites the table definition, not the records).
DEFINE TABLE OVERWRITE relation SCHEMAFULL TYPE RELATION FROM entity TO entity;

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE relation_type ON relation TYPE string;
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE properties ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE object DEFAULT {};

-- Provenance
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE source_documents ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT [];
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE extracted_at ON relation TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE extraction_method ON relation TYPE string DEFAULT "llm";
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE confidence ON relation TYPE float DEFAULT 1.0;
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE provenance_chain ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT [];

-- Status
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE status ON relation TYPE string DEFAULT "active";
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE created_at ON relation TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();

-- Indexes
DEFINE INDEX OVERWRITE idx_relation_type ON relation FIELDS relation_type;
DEFINE INDEX OVERWRITE idx_relation_status ON relation FIELDS status;
